============================================================
                 HEAP & PRIORITY QUEUE IN C++
============================================================

1. WHAT IS A HEAP?
------------------------------------------------------------

A Heap is a special tree-based data structure.

A heap follows a special property between a parent and
its children.

There are mainly two types:

    1. Min Heap
    2. Max Heap


MAX HEAP:

    Parent >= Children


MIN HEAP:

    Parent <= Children


Example of Max Heap:

             50
           /    \
         30      40
        /  \    /  \
      10   20  25   35


Every parent is greater than or equal to its children.


Example of Min Heap:

             10
           /    \
         20      15
        /  \    /  \
      30   25  40   35


Every parent is smaller than or equal to its children.



2. COMPLETE BINARY TREE
------------------------------------------------------------

A Heap is a Complete Binary Tree.

Complete Binary Tree means:

    All levels are completely filled
    except possibly the last level.

The last level is filled from:

    LEFT -> RIGHT


Example:

             10
           /    \
         20      30
        /  \    /
      40   50  60

array : {10, 20, 30, 40, 50 , 60 , NULL};


This is a Complete Binary Tree.


This matters because a heap can be stored efficiently
inside an array/vector.

We don't need actual tree nodes.



3. HEAP USING ARRAY
------------------------------------------------------------

Suppose the heap is:

             50
           /    \
         30      40
        /  \    /
      10   20  25


Store it as:

    [50, 30, 40, 10, 20, 25]


Now we can represent the whole heap using an array.

This is one of the most important properties of a heap.



4. INDEXING IN A HEAP
------------------------------------------------------------

Suppose indexing starts from 0.

For a node at index i:

    Left Child  = 2*i + 1

    Right Child = 2*i + 2

    Parent      = (i - 1) / 2


Example:

    index = 1

    Left Child:

        2*1 + 1 = 3

    Right Child:

        2*1 + 2 = 4

    Parent:

        (1 - 1) / 2 = 0


So:

             index 0
              50
             /  \
            /    \
       index 1   index 2
          30       40
         /  \
        /    \
     index3 index4
       10      20



5. MAX HEAP
------------------------------------------------------------

In a Max Heap:

    Parent >= Children


Example:

             100
            /   \
          70     80
         / \    / \
       40  50  60  30


The largest element is always at the root.

Therefore:

    MAX HEAP
        |
        v
    Maximum element at top



6. MIN HEAP
------------------------------------------------------------

In a Min Heap:

    Parent <= Children


Example:

             10
            /  \
          20    30
         / \   / \
       40  50 60 70


The smallest element is always at the root.

Therefore:

    MIN HEAP
        |
        v
    Minimum element at top



7. WHY DO WE USE HEAPS?
------------------------------------------------------------

A heap is useful when we repeatedly need:

    Maximum element

or

    Minimum element


Common examples:

    - Priority Queue
    - K largest elements
    - K smallest elements
    - Scheduling
    - Top K problems
    - Dijkstra
    - Prim's Algorithm
    - Merge K sorted arrays
    - Median problems
    - Greedy algorithms



8. PRIORITY QUEUE
------------------------------------------------------------

A Priority Queue is a data structure where the element
with the highest priority is removed first.

In C++, priority_queue is generally implemented using
a heap.

Default:

    priority_queue

behaves like a:

    MAX HEAP


Example:

    priority_queue<int> pq;


Insert:

    pq.push(10);
    pq.push(30);
    pq.push(20);


The largest element has highest priority.

So:

    top() -> 30



9. BASIC PRIORITY_QUEUE OPERATIONS
------------------------------------------------------------

Create:

    priority_queue<int> pq;


Insert:

    pq.push(x);


Get highest priority element:

    pq.top();


Remove highest priority element:

    pq.pop();

Number of elements:

    pq.size();



IMPORTANT:

    top()
    does NOT remove the element.

    pop()
    removes the top element.



10. MAX HEAP EXAMPLE
------------------------------------------------------------

Code:

#include <bits/stdc++.h>
using namespace std;

int main() {

    priority_queue<int> pq;

    pq.push(10);
    pq.push(50);
    pq.push(20);
    pq.push(40);
    pq.push(30);

    cout << pq.top() << '\n';

    return 0;
}


Output:

    50


Because priority_queue is a Max Heap by default.



11. POPPING FROM MAX HEAP
------------------------------------------------------------

Example:

    priority_queue<int> pq;

    pq.push(10);
    pq.push(50);
    pq.push(20);
    pq.push(40);
    pq.push(30);


Now:

    pq.top() = 50


After:

    pq.pop();


Top becomes:

    40


After another pop:

    30


Then:

    20

    10


So the values come out in:

    50 40 30 20 10



12. PRINT ALL ELEMENTS
------------------------------------------------------------

You can repeatedly pop elements:

    while (!pq.empty()) {

        cout << pq.top() << ' ';

        pq.pop();
    }


For a Max Heap, output will be in descending order.

Example:

    50 40 30 20 10


IMPORTANT:

This destroys the priority queue.

So if you need the original pq later,
make a copy first.



13. MIN HEAP
------------------------------------------------------------

C++ can also create a Min Heap.

Syntax:

    priority_queue<
        int,
        vector<int>,
        greater<int>
    > pq;


Now:

    pq.top()

gives the smallest element.


Example:

    priority_queue<
        int,
        vector<int>,
        greater<int>
    > pq;

    pq.push(30);
    pq.push(10);
    pq.push(20);
    pq.push(5);


Now:

    pq.top()

is:

    5



14. MAX HEAP VS MIN HEAP
------------------------------------------------------------

MAX HEAP:

    priority_queue<int> pq;

    top() -> maximum


MIN HEAP:

    priority_queue<
        int,
        vector<int>,
        greater<int>
    > pq;

    top() -> minimum


Remember:

    Default priority_queue
        -> Max Heap


    priority_queue + greater
        -> Min Heap



15. HEAP COMPLEXITY
------------------------------------------------------------

For a heap containing N elements:

    push()  -> O(log N)

    pop()   -> O(log N)

    top()   -> O(1)

    size()  -> O(1)


Why is push/pop O(log N)?

Because the height of a complete binary tree is:

    O(log N)



16. WHY IS top() O(1)?
------------------------------------------------------------

In a Max Heap:

    maximum element is always at root.


In a Min Heap:

    minimum element is always at root.


Therefore:

    top()

only needs to look at the root.

So:

    top() -> O(1)



17. INSERTING INTO A HEAP
------------------------------------------------------------

Suppose we have a Max Heap:

             50
            /  \
          30    40
         / \
       10   20


Insert:

    60


First put 60 at the next available position:

             50
            /  \
          30    40
         / \    /
       10  20  60


Now heap property is broken.

60 > 40

So swap:

             50
            /  \
          30    60
         / \    /
       10  20  40


Again:

    60 > 50

Swap again:

             60
            /  \
          30    50
         / \    /
       10  20  40


Now the heap property is restored.

This process is called:

    UPHEAP
    or
    BUBBLE UP



18. DELETING ROOT FROM A HEAP
------------------------------------------------------------

Suppose Max Heap:

             50
            /  \
          30    40
         / \    /
       10  20  25


Remove 50.

We generally:

    1. Take the last element
    2. Put it at root
    3. Fix the heap


Move 25 to root:

             25
            /  \
          30    40
         / \
       10  20


Heap property is broken.

25 < 40


Swap with the larger child:

             40
            /  \
          30    25
         / \
       10  20


Heap property is restored.

This process is called:

    DOWNHEAP
    or
    BUBBLE DOWN



19. HEAPIFY
------------------------------------------------------------

Heapify means fixing a heap.

There are two common directions:

    1. Upward
    2. Downward


UPHEAP:

    Used after insertion


DOWNHEAP:

    Used after deleting the root



20. BUILDING A HEAP
------------------------------------------------------------

Suppose:

    a = [10, 50, 20, 40, 30]


We can build a heap from this array.

In C++, priority_queue can be constructed easily.

Example:

    vector<int> a = {10, 50, 20, 40, 30};

    priority_queue<int> pq(
        a.begin(),
        a.end()
    );


Now pq is a Max Heap.

    pq.top() = 50



21. HEAP SORT IDEA
------------------------------------------------------------

A heap can be used for sorting.

For a Max Heap:

    repeatedly remove maximum


Example:

    50 40 30 20 10


For a Min Heap:

    repeatedly remove minimum


Example:

    10 20 30 40 50


This gives us Heap Sort.



22. PRIORITY QUEUE WITH PAIRS
------------------------------------------------------------

priority_queue can store pairs.

Example:

    priority_queue<pair<int, int>> pq;


Suppose:

    pq.push({10, 100});
    pq.push({30, 200});
    pq.push({20, 300});


By default pairs are compared lexicographically.

So the largest first value gets priority.

Top:

    {30, 200}



23. IMPORTANT PAIR BEHAVIOR
------------------------------------------------------------

For:

    pair<int, int>


Comparison works like:

    first value
        |
        v
    compare first

If first values are equal:

    compare second


Example:

    {10, 50}
    {10, 30}


Then:

    {10, 50}

comes first.



24. MIN HEAP OF PAIRS
------------------------------------------------------------

Example:

    priority_queue<
        pair<int, int>,
        vector<pair<int, int>>,
        greater<pair<int, int>>
    > pq;


Now the smallest pair comes first.


Example:

    pq.push({5, 100});
    pq.push({2, 200});
    pq.push({7, 50});


Top:

    {2, 200}



25. MOST COMMON CP USE CASE
------------------------------------------------------------

Suppose we repeatedly need the smallest element.

Instead of sorting every time:

    sort(a.begin(), a.end());


we can use:

    Min Heap


Then:

    pq.top()

gives the smallest current element.


This is useful when elements are continuously
being inserted and removed.



26. EXAMPLE: K LARGEST ELEMENTS
------------------------------------------------------------

Suppose:

    a = {10, 5, 20, 8, 15, 30}

Find the largest K elements.

Suppose:

    K = 3


Answer:

    30 20 15


One approach is sorting:

    O(N log N)


But heap gives another powerful approach.

Use a Min Heap of size K.

Keep only the K largest elements.



27. K LARGEST ELEMENTS USING MIN HEAP
------------------------------------------------------------

Idea:

    Maintain a Min Heap containing K elements.


For every x:

    push x

If heap size > K:

    pop smallest


Why?

Because the smallest among our K candidates
is the least useful one.


Code:

#include <bits/stdc++.h>
using namespace std;

int main() {

    vector<int> a = {10, 5, 20, 8, 15, 30};

    int k = 3;

    priority_queue<
        int,
        vector<int>,
        greater<int>
    > pq;

    for (int x : a) {

        pq.push(x);

        if (pq.size() > k) {
            pq.pop();
        }
    }

    while (!pq.empty()) {

        cout << pq.top() << ' ';

        pq.pop();
    }

    return 0;
}


The heap contains:

    15 20 30



28. K SMALLEST ELEMENTS
------------------------------------------------------------

For K smallest elements, use a:

    Max Heap


Example:

    a = {10, 5, 20, 8, 15, 30}

    K = 3


Answer:

    5 8 10


Maintain a Max Heap of size K.

For every x:

    push x

If size > K:

    pop largest


This keeps the K smallest elements.



29. IMPORTANT RULE FOR TOP K
------------------------------------------------------------

This is very important.


K LARGEST:

    Use MIN HEAP of size K


K SMALLEST:

    Use MAX HEAP of size K


Remember:

    Want to keep largest values
        -> remove the smallest
        -> MIN HEAP


    Want to keep smallest values
        -> remove the largest
        -> MAX HEAP



30. HEAP FOR FREQUENCY PROBLEMS
------------------------------------------------------------

Heap is often combined with hashing.

Example:

    Find K most frequent elements.


First:

    unordered_map<int, int> freq;


Count frequencies.

Then use a heap to keep the top K.



Concept:

    HASHING
        +
    HEAP
        =
    TOP K PROBLEMS



31. PRIORITY QUEUE VS QUEUE
------------------------------------------------------------

Normal queue:

    First In First Out

Example:

    10 -> 20 -> 30

Remove:

    10


Priority Queue:

    Highest priority comes first.

Example Max Heap:

    10
    50
    20
    30

Remove:

    50


So:

    queue
        -> order of arrival


    priority_queue
        -> priority of element



32. PRIORITY QUEUE VS STACK
------------------------------------------------------------

Stack:

    Last In First Out


Priority Queue:

    Highest priority first


Example:

Stack:

    push 10
    push 20
    push 30

pop:

    30


Priority Queue:

    push 10
    push 20
    push 30

Max Heap pop:

    30


They may produce the same result in some cases,
but their concepts are different.



33. CUSTOM COMPARATOR
------------------------------------------------------------

Sometimes we need custom priority.

For example:

    smaller value should have higher priority


We can use:

    greater<int>


For more complex objects, use a comparator.


Example:

    struct cmp {

        bool operator()(
            pair<int,int> a,
            pair<int,int> b
        ) {

            return a.second > b.second;
        }
    };


Then:

    priority_queue<
        pair<int,int>,
        vector<pair<int,int>>,
        cmp
    > pq;



